-- By construction: a handler's type is computed from its pattern, so a wrong-arity or wrong-type handler fails to compile, and `PathSeg.lit` and `MountPoint` take a proof that no segment is `.` or `..`. The `#guard_msgs` tests in [test/RoutingTests/](test/RoutingTests/) pin these, along with the compile-time rejection of malformed patterns, duplicate route names and captured mount prefixes.
+- By construction: a handler's type is computed from its pattern, so a wrong-arity or wrong-type handler fails to compile. `PathSeg.lit` takes a proof that its segment is not `.` or `..`, and `MountPoint` one that no segment is empty, `.` or `..`. The `#guard_msgs` tests in [test/RoutingTests/](test/RoutingTests/) pin these, along with the compile-time rejection of malformed patterns, duplicate route names and captured mount prefixes.
 
